Research: .collab/research/2026-10-16-t3-code-c2c-support.md and live receipt JSON. Quality: preserve existing codebase architecture; small typed interfaces; meaningful regression and failure tests; no mock-only delivery claims; no whole-tree refactor; independent review/fix loop before merge. Decisions and evidence must be appended through bl. Integrate T3 run scheduler and mailbox. Stable identity across resume/restart/unload; exact routing on same-workspace threads and native/T3 children; at most one batched local-only eligible wake; DND, snooze, stop, archive/delete and pending approvals respected. Keep raw peer payload away from user-message and approval APIs. Bind/unbind event watcher with lifecycle; recover safely after crashes.

Implementation decision: C2cService depends on ThreadManagement; Orchestrator and ProviderTurnStartService depend only on low-level C2cMailbox to avoid cycles. A shared ThreadCommandExecutor lock must cover last-moment claim/lifecycle validation AND provider submission. A boolean precheck alone leaves a Stop race. Rejected prepared runs need conditional cancellation so they cannot remain phantom-running. Timed DND and snooze need deadline-driven reconciliation; broker and projection events drive other wakeups. Peer payload stays solely in run.peerData, with a system timeline marker and host-owned sender/messageId metadata; ordinary dispatch must not clear snooze/settled/Stop holds.

Cross-broker workspace moves: v1 must fail closed before creating a replacement registration, preserving the previous binding/inbox for recovery. Same-origin worktree moves remain supported. Use an expected-broker-root precondition on host bind for existing bindings; do not silently overwrite the only old broker reference or claim transparent migration. Moving a bound thread to an unrelated repository requires a new thread for now, with an actionable status. Startup must recover in-flight claims synchronously before EffectWorker activation.

Implementation lanes (after host gate and claim): one shared T3 integration worktree because this is one dependent lifecycle slice. Lifecycle lane owns Orchestrator/EventSink/ProviderTurnStartService/RunExecutionService/command schemas and corresponding tests; mailbox lane owns C2cService plus low-level mailbox/bridge extensions; coordinator owns shared full-ancestry locks, compact delivery-state/policy gate, bounded projection queries, and runtime composition. Interfaces agreed before edits; no whole-tree refactor. Full ancestor root-to-target locking prevents parent Stop races. Stop transaction pauses enrolled descendants even if root has no binding. Delivery reads must include held queued runs (shell idle alone is insufficient). All tests run on x-p35, focused T3 scope only; independent Terra review then integration review before merge.

Durability decision from production SQLite inspection: T3 uses WAL synchronous=NORMAL, which is process-crash safe but can lose accepted rows on OS/power failure. Broker ACK therefore needs an awaited durability barrier after mailbox.accept COMMIT and before ACK, including deduplicated accepts. Extend existing Node SQLite checkpoint worker with correlated PASSIVE request/result; succeed only busy=0, log>=0, checkpointed=log, checkpoint_fullfsync enabled. Partial/busy/worker failure/timeout withholds ACK. Memory tests use explicit no-disk implementation; file-backed clients fail closed if checkpoint unavailable. Preserve shared SqlClient/Stop transactions and global NORMAL performance policy. Primary references: sqlite.org/pragma.html#pragma_synchronous and sqlite.org/c3ref/wal_checkpoint_v2.html. No extra queue/database or periodic mailbox polling.

Review decisions: lifecycle uses one root-first app-owned ancestry lock order across Stop, active injection and final start; native children are rejected before host enrollment. Explicit precommit peer-data rejection is distinguishable from ambiguous commit failure so only known-unsent claims are released. Full server typecheck passes on integration worktree; runtime verification is underway on x-p35. New awaited SQLite durability barrier has all nine focused cases passing; an existing large-value SQLite test is under baseline investigation.

Architecture refinement: orchestration only validates cached SQL claim and T3 eligibility when accepting a pending peer run. The external host refresh belongs at the actual provider-effect boundary (and active injection), preserving pure event decisions and avoiding redundant subprocess work. Pending acceptance is not authorization to submit. Independent review fixes include native-resume history restoration, attempt-fenced cleanup for known-unsent startup exits, and refreshed host state after asynchronous history injection.

Final review corrections: terminal operational provider failures hold mail, even when known-unsent, to prevent failed-run events from scheduling an unbounded retry loop; only local lifecycle eligibility rejection releases automatically. Peer provider effects have a shared 30-second deadline while ancestor locks are held, so a silent native RPC cannot block Stop indefinitely; timeout remains ambiguous/held. Tests cover these paths with deterministic Effect clock and lock reacquisition.

Implemented and locally merged T3 lifecycle commit 624557187eb664e650b6c90aaf29c9c28a87cd37 (source tree 6d50f420b4330348dae1b645d0ede95a1a85df10). Independent review PASS. Exact-source x-p35 Node 24.22 validation: 407 unique tests across 14 focused files, plus three passing watcher-recovery stability repetitions; server/contracts/shared typechecks and targeted lint pass. Receipt /tmp/t3-c2c-validation-deps/integration-final-validation-receipt.json on x-p35, source digest 4ebaab3c8be4645d4656bfb5f8e3111edbe736fb4fc80edbb9d58e0ece6fc811. Terminal failures and defects hold mail without retry loops; native peer effects are bounded to 30s; Stop, DND, snooze, identity isolation and recovery are covered. Real isolated end-to-end proof remains E2.T004 after tool/configuration surface E2.T003.